#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb820969-b00e-58fd-9e6d-f8c786f91b33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c54cf95-8d93-5f06-addc-038acebc5019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2592" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484841} {:answer/id #uuid "0c65b508-2845-571b-8454-76c6030fae62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2952" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484842} {:answer/id #uuid "48a6a32b-b9ab-5bec-85a1-8073a3608631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2529" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484974} {:answer/id #uuid "5814f8c1-9cae-53cc-8145-d58f8cc938cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2552" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485016} {:answer/id #uuid "668716e1-89c4-559b-bcb0-1a077d43cacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2599" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c54cf95-8d93-5f06-addc-038acebc5019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2592" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484841} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd2f20a1-a54d-5ed7-a2a8-fb23d83de889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b831793d-1ec0-54c8-869c-6f86de445418" :question/math-academy-id "q-132546" :question/problem "Vehicles arrive at a crossing continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $10$ seconds. Determine the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing within the next $3$ seconds. Round your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the time intervals between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing are continuous and independent, and arrivals occur at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next vehicle arrives as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing. Then, since vehicles arrive every $10$ seconds on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/10$ vehicles per second.\n\nThe time between arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =1/10,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing within the next $3$ seconds is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/10}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3/10}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-3/10} \\\\  & ≈0.2592\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e40fd627-b52a-5d60-b144-2087979533fe" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87cb0385-7440-5272-98a7-928a22fd8042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.670" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87cb0385-7440-5272-98a7-928a22fd8042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.670" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485112} :answer-field/id #uuid "95f02d2d-c10d-5ca4-b6d9-0139b28173a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13a56b51-6b01-5125-b67e-75c6aab58d86" :question/math-academy-id "q-253892" :question/problem "At a particular helpdesk, customer waiting times occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $6$ customers per hour. If a customer has already waited for $6$ minutes, what is the probability that they will need to wait at least $4$ more minutes for their call to be answered? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since waiting times occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call is answered using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the customer's waiting time, in minutes. Since the help desk serves $6$ customers per hour on average (or $1$ customer every $10$ minutes), the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ customers per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ $s=6,$ and $t=4.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6+4\\,|\\,X>6) & =P(X>4) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/10)\\cdot (4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2/5} \\\\  & ≈0.670.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e40fd627-b52a-5d60-b144-2087979533fe" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5baebaba-fb25-532c-a3b0-5a6c14ea2ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.264" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5baebaba-fb25-532c-a3b0-5a6c14ea2ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.264" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485020} :answer-field/id #uuid "938386cd-7f18-57c1-8aef-c0067eb1a186" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b588bb0d-6d8e-5a2d-ae2c-642f8c01ed06" :question/math-academy-id "q-254586" :question/problem "A fisherman catches fish continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $1$ fish every $15$ minutes. If the fisherman has already been fishing for $30$ minutes without catching any fish, what is the probability that he will need to fish for at least $20$ more minutes before catching his first fish? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since catching fish occurs continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next catch using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the time until the next catch, in minutes. Since the fisherman catches $1$ fish every $15$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15}$ fish per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{15}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15},$ $s=30,$ and $t=20.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>30+20\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X>20) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/15)\\cdot (20)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4/3} \\\\  & ≈0.264.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb820969-b00e-58fd-9e6d-f8c786f91b33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e33eb3c-cec7-5493-9f32-c984010c4bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0302" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484871} {:answer/id #uuid "275b76d2-5fd6-553c-b3f2-cd785fa8f998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9670" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484889} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7f8381-9c80-59cc-9eb2-c3583a9235be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2485" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485128} {:answer/id #uuid "c29e1a84-9ffd-505b-8a79-6dcd87d514f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4353" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485233} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ea36b6-b0d0-5067-953e-f4ef81d92a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7515" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f7f8381-9c80-59cc-9eb2-c3583a9235be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2485" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485128} :answer-field/id #uuid "a99fb244-28cb-58fa-857b-0ab86cacbcc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99aa1c5a-eed9-5ae6-b8c6-e2b13db7c0f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96367" :question/problem "Mark runs a hotel near a popular tourist resort. He knows that, on average, people usually book their room $3.5$ days before their trip. By modeling the number of days between the customer's booking and arrival as continuous and assuming that these numbers are independent, determine the probability that the next customer books their room less than $1$ full day in advance. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the time interval between the customer's booking and arrival is continuous and the time of different bookings are independent and occur at a constant average rate, we can model the time between the next customer's booking and arrival using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of days between a booking and the customer's arrival. Since customers book their room $3.5=7/2$ days in advance on average, $X$ can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =2/7,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{2}{7}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7}{e}^{-2x/7}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a customer books a room less than $1$ full day in advance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{7}{e}^{-2x/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x/7}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/7}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.2485\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "faf45d2f-484a-56ee-af3e-c14a63721c19" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb7dd9eb-cdeb-501a-a394-258b4c0a70a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171448} {:answer/id #uuid "1b166edc-f9ee-54cf-a9dd-16b3169ff4a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0111" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171449} {:answer/id #uuid "1e94cdee-8432-5001-bc67-ae18a90f7237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0498" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171450} {:answer/id #uuid "268664a8-6e49-52a3-8c87-9405b615b645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171451} {:answer/id #uuid "339dc04c-c725-5bea-bd34-5df67317f404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7165" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e94cdee-8432-5001-bc67-ae18a90f7237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0498" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171450} :answer-field/id #uuid "02f76e3c-1245-5939-a1bc-92a2d23477ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec6131e7-de2b-527c-b4ba-ef95dae870b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-96381" :question/problem "At a call center, the time taken for phone calls to be answered varies continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $3$ calls every minute. What is the probability of taking more than $1$ minute to answer the next call? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the time until the next call is answered occurs continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call is answered using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time, in minutes, between calls. Then, since there are $3$ calls per minute on average, the rate is $\\lambda =3$ calls per minute.\n\nThe amount of time before the next call can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =3,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next call will occur after $1$ minute or more is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈0.0498.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
